# Troubleshoot your first trace

Most first-trace problems come down to the API key, the region, or where the run is recorded. Work through these in order.

## The run never appears

- **The key is missing.** The TypeScript SDK prints `UNCOIL_API_KEY is not set` and records nothing. The Python SDK raises a `ValueError` when the client is created. Set `UNCOIL_API_KEY` in the environment where the agent actually runs, not just in your shell.
- **The key and region disagree.** A key belongs to the EU or US region. If the configured region does not match, the client refuses to start with `Uncoil API key region mismatch`. Set `region` (or the `UNCOIL_REGION` variable) to the region your project lives in. Keys without a region marker are treated as EU.
- **Nothing ran inside a traced function.** `traceAgent` (or `@trace_agent`) creates the run. Calls made outside one are recorded as separate standalone roots, so look for many one-step traces instead of one run.
- **A request failed quietly.** Telemetry never throws into your agent, so failures show up as a `Failed to ingest span` warning in your logs. Check the log output of the process that runs the agent.
- **The endpoint is wrong.** Unless you set `host` (or `UNCOIL_HOST`), the SDK sends spans to `/api/ingest` on the host for your region. A `host` that is not a full web address fails with `bad_host`. A custom host that is not an Uncoil region host skips the region check, so confirm it by hand. If the process cannot reach the host you will see `Could not reach Uncoil` in your logs; check your network, firewall and proxy.
- **The key was rejected.** A 401 is reported as `Uncoil rejected the API key`, and a 403 as `This API key is not allowed to send data`. Check the key, its region, and that it belongs to the project you are looking at.
- **The process exited too early.** Both SDKs send spans in the background so tracing never slows your agent down. A short script, serverless handler or worker that is frozen between requests can stop before the last spans are sent. Wait for them before returning:
  - TypeScript: `await flush()` waits for every pending span, up to 5 seconds by default (`await uncoil.flush()` waits for one client). It resolves to `false` if some spans were still sending when the time ran out.
  - Python: `uncoil.flush(timeout=5)` waits for every client (`client.flush()` waits for one) and returns `False` if spans were still waiting. Spans still queued when the program exits get up to two seconds to send. Up to 1,000 spans can wait at once; past that, new spans are dropped and reported once with the code `queue_full`.
- **The run was sampled out.** See the next section.

## The run is there, but steps are missing or flat

- Steps must be started inside the traced function, using `span`, `observe`, or a wrapped provider client. Work started in a detached process or an unrelated queue consumer has no parent.
- If steps appear but not nested, make sure the work is awaited inside the `span` callback (or inside the `with` block in Python). A step ends when its function settles.

## Runs are missing because of sampling

If you set `sampleRate` (`sample_rate` in Python) below 1, only that share of runs is recorded. The choice is made per run, so a kept run is complete and a dropped run leaves nothing behind. Spans that report an error are still kept unless you set `keepErrors` (`keep_errors`) to `false`. A value outside 0 to 1 throws `bad_sample_rate`. Set it back to 1 while you debug.

## Too much data (rate limits)

When Uncoil answers 429, the SDK reports `Uncoil is receiving too much data from this app`, and the spans sent at that moment are lost. Lower the volume with `sampleRate` (for example 0.2), or upgrade your plan. Each kind of problem is reported once per client. Pass `onError` to receive these problems in your own code instead of a console warning.

## Verify ingestion

Run the setup check from the TypeScript SDK package:

```bash
npx @uncoil.dev/sdk doctor
```

It checks the key, the region and host, and the network, then sends one test trace. It exits with code 1 if a check fails, and prints the run id of the test trace when it is accepted, which you can search for in your dashboard. Use `--no-send` to skip the test trace, or `--region` and `--host` to override the defaults. For a quick local check, set `onError` so that a rejected or unreachable request is visible.

## Cost shows as zero

Uncoil does not guess prices. Provider wrappers store token usage but set cost to zero and mark the span `costKnown` false until you supply a `calculateCost` function (`calculate_cost` in Python) with your own rate card. See [OpenAI, Anthropic and LangGraph](/docs/provider-integrations). For manual spans, pass `costUsd` yourself.

## A model call appears twice

You are recording the same call through two paths, usually a wrapped provider client plus a framework's model callbacks, or a wrapped client plus a manual `llmCall`. Keep one.

## Inputs or outputs look cut off or redacted

Inputs and outputs are capped at 10,000 characters, and obvious secrets such as bearer tokens, API keys, JWTs and `password=` values are replaced with a redaction marker before they leave your process. This is intentional. If you need less in the trace, set `captureInput` or `captureOutput` to `false` on a provider wrapper, or call `observe` with `captureInput: false`.

## Still stuck

Check [Choose an Uncoil Cloud region](/docs/data-regions) for the endpoints each region uses, and review [what is in a trace](/docs/trace-anatomy) to confirm what you expect to see.
